{
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "bin_center",
      "description": "At initialization, the hand was high above an unobstructed bin and both calibrated views showed its opening. The policy repeatedly wrote unknown while the annotation held a reconstructed center. This is a PREDICTABILITY failure in applying the acceptance criterion, not a missing historical fact. Clarify that unknown is reserved for genuinely underconstrained geometry, not ordinary pixel-estimation uncertainty. In these clear two-view situations, match the inner opening's corners in cyclic order, triangulate corresponding corners using the two camera calibrations, and check that they form a horizontal rectangle whose reprojections agree with the visible inner rim. Compute the xy center from its opposing corners. When the plausible corner fits constrain the center within 1 cm, write that center immediately; an example is [0.024, -0.095]. Exact pixel agreement is not required. Do not match an outer-rim corner to an inner-rim corner. Retain the existing initialization, preservation, correction, and displacement rules otherwise."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "At initial cube approaches, small top faces were localized with enough bias to put the chosen waypoint more than 1 cm from the recorded target. Similar numerical localization error occurred on an approach to the stop button after all quotas were complete. These are SUFFICIENCY failures of geometric estimation, not evidence that another historical field is needed. Strengthen the existing localization procedure: for a cube, identify the four top-face corners rather than the centroid of the whole colored patch, intersect their calibrated rays with z approximately 0.04, and fit a square in world coordinates. Use the fitted square's center for xy and its world-space edges for yaw. When both views show the face, check the same fitted square against both views, rejecting a fit biased toward a visible side face. This applies especially to the small cube faces in the initial high wrist view. For the button, fit the gray circular top in its approximately z=0.02 plane and verify the resulting center against the available second view; do not use the square base or a rounded whole-object centroid. Keep the existing approach heights, gripper commands, and approach-to-descent target continuity. For initial bin localization, explicitly use the memory instruction's two-view corner-fit acceptance test: normal estimation uncertainty does not justify a visibility-recovery motion when the center is already constrained within 1 cm. Do not change deposit counting, release, retreat, or completion behavior.",
  "refused": "The reports correctly expose an unresolved choice among multiple accessible eligible cubes, both initially and after retreats. However, they do not establish a common selection rule that reproduces the recorded choices. Suggested rules such as robot-nearest conflict with the supplied world coordinates and with other selections: the robot is on the negative-x side, so a foreground cube with larger x is not generally the robot-nearest cube. Other reports request different image extrema or a middle foreground cube without giving a common criterion. The goal specifies color quotas, not cube identity or order, and no earlier observable selection establishes these later targets. I therefore do not pass on episode-specific coordinates, image-pixel targets, a count-indexed target schedule, or an invented nearest/rightmost priority as a general correction. The selection SUFFICIENCY problem remains unresolved by the supplied evidence; reproducing those exact choices requires a supported selection convention rather than additional historical memory. Likewise, requests for particular yaw representatives among equivalent square-edge grasps do not establish a consistent convention, and the stated sufficiency comparison concerns position and gripper state, not those equivalent orientations. The existing instruction already permits unrestricted feasible edge-aligned yaw. The bin annotation itself needs no change: it already requires reconstruction from geometry available at that cycle and forbids hindsight or action-destination substitution; only the policy's acceptance guidance needs clarification. Keep deposited_green conditionally defined, not merged or deleted: none of the supplied goals requests green, so the existing rule omits it and its lack of reads is expected. It carries no value in these episodes; when green is requested, its independent quota cannot be represented by a blue or red count. The reports with no identified missing information do not justify new state or episode-specific waypoint corrections."
}

optimizer.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "kind": "modify",
      "field": "bin_center",
      "edits": [
        {
          "modify_from": "When this cycle's calibrated views reliably constrain the center using the action instruction's geometric reconstruction and reprojection checks, write that center.",
          "modify_to": "When this cycle's calibrated views reliably constrain the center using the action instruction's geometric reconstruction and reprojection checks, write that center immediately. Unknown is reserved for genuinely underconstrained geometry, not ordinary pixel-estimation uncertainty. When both views clearly show the opening, identify corresponding inner-rim corners in consistent cyclic order, triangulate them using the respective camera calibrations, and fit a horizontal rectangle. Check its reprojections against the visible inner rim and compute its world xy center from opposing corners. Never match an outer-rim corner to an inner-rim corner. Accept the fit when plausible corner fits constrain the center within 0.01 m in each world coordinate; exact pixel agreement is not required. This two-view test does not exclude the action instruction's geometrically constrained single-view reconstruction."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Cubes are approximately 4 cm wide: identify and back-project their top-face centers at approximately z=0.04 and use the resulting world xy for approach and the z=0.02 grasp. Use the top face, not the whole visible silhouette; visible side faces must not bias the center.",
      "modify_to": "Cubes are approximately 4 cm wide: identify their four top-face corners, intersect the calibrated corner rays with the plane at approximately z=0.04, and fit a square in world coordinates. Use the fitted square's center for approach xy and the z=0.02 grasp, and its world-space edges for feasible edge-aligned yaw. When both views show the face, check the same fitted square against both views. Apply this procedure also to small faces in a high wrist view. Use the top face, not the whole colored patch or visible silhouette; reject fits biased toward a visible side face. Preserve the existing approached-cube identity and orientation through descent and lift."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Prefer reliable two-view correspondences between actual inner-rim corners or edge intersections: triangulate them and fit their common horizontal plane.",
      "modify_to": "Prefer reliable two-view correspondences between actual inner-rim corners or edge intersections: triangulate them and fit their common horizontal plane. For a clearly visible opening, match inner-rim corners in consistent cyclic order and apply the bin_center memory instruction's horizontal-rectangle fit and reprojection acceptance test. Do not match inner-rim corners to outer-rim corners. Ordinary pixel-estimation uncertainty does not justify unknown or a visibility-recovery motion when plausible fits already constrain the center within 0.01 m in each world coordinate; exact pixel agreement is not required."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Locate the button by its gray circular top on a light square base; back-project the top center at approximately z=0.02 and use that xy for approach and press.",
      "modify_to": "Locate the button by its gray circular top on a light square base. Fit the circular top in its world plane at approximately z=0.02 using calibrated rays from its visible boundary, and verify the fitted circle's reprojection against the other current view when available. Use the fitted world xy center for approach, empty-gripper closing, and press; do not use the square base or a rounded whole-object centroid."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "When already aligned above the button with open fingers, re-localize the gray circular button-top center, back-projecting it at approximately z=0.02 and checking the other current view when visible. Use the circular top, not the surrounding square base.",
      "modify_to": "When already aligned above the button with open fingers, re-localize the gray circular button-top center using the calibrated circle fit in the approximately z=0.02 plane and its available second-view reprojection check. Use the circular top, not the surrounding square base."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "No episode-specific coordinates, image-pixel targets, count-indexed target schedule, fixed color priority, or nearest/rightmost cube ranking is added. Across rounds, the proposed rankings conflict and no supported general selection convention has been established; additional memory cannot predict arbitrary future recorded choices. No yaw representative convention or yaw-history field is added. No cube or button position memory is justified by current-view estimation errors. The bin_center annotation remains unchanged: its observed-geometry timing and prohibition on hindsight remain valid. The new acceptance guidance preserves both the single-view fallback and retained-center use established in earlier rounds; it does not restore mandatory two-camera reconstruction. Deposit counts, including conditionally present deposited_green, and held_color remain unchanged."
}
